    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Research objective</h2>

      <p>
        Plant-health decisions depend on environmental conditions that change
        continuously, but conventional observation often relies on
        disconnected measurements and manual inspection. Important changes in
        moisture, temperature, humidity, light, or gas conditions may therefore
        be difficult to identify early or evaluate in their temporal context.
      </p>

      <p>
        PlantLife365 was developed to validate an AI-powered multimodal sensing
        and data-collection platform for plant health management. The project
        connects physical sensing with secure software infrastructure so that
        environmental measurements can be collected, organized, visualized,
        analyzed, and converted into information that supports timely
        monitoring decisions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Multimodal sensing hardware</h2>

      <p>
        The edge node is built around an ESP32 microcontroller and acquires
        temperature, humidity, soil-moisture, ambient-light, and gas
        measurements, with support for optional image capture. The integrated
        sensor package provides complementary information about the plant and
        its surrounding environment rather than relying on a single
        measurement modality.
      </p>

      <p>
        MicroPython firmware manages sensor acquisition and transfers telemetry
        to the web platform. Each registered hardware device uses a device
        secret, and incoming payloads are checked for required fields, numeric
        validity, finite values, and application-level ranges before they are
        stored.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Authenticated monitoring and data services</h2>

      <p>
        A Django application provides the persistence, data-access, and
        monitoring layers. Measurements remain associated with the
        authenticated user's registered devices, allowing current readings,
        historical records, rolling and daily statistics, alerts, system logs,
        and exported data to be managed within a user-scoped workflow.
      </p>

      <p>
        The dashboard presents live sensor conditions and temporal trends in a
        browser-based interface. Users can review alert states, filter
        historical records, examine changes across sensors, and export their
        data for further analysis.
      </p>
    </section>

